          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Do bodies disagree about using a short-acting reliever on its own in asthma?",
          "answer": "Current guidelines strongly discourage using short-acting relievers (such as short-acting bronchodilators) alone for asthma management. According to the Global Strategy for Asthma Management, every individual with asthma should receive inhaled corticosteroid-containing therapy. Short-acting relievers alone leave inflammation untreated, potentially leading to severe exacerbations, even in those with infrequent symptoms (source: \"Asthma: confirming the diagnosis and the stepwise approach to controller therapy\").\n\nMany health authorities emphasize that treating asthma solely with short-acting relievers is not appropriate at any severity. Heavy use of these relievers can indicate a higher risk for exacerbations, reinforcing the need for comprehensive management instead of relying on short-acting medication (source: \"Asthma: confirming the diagnosis and the stepwise approach to controller therapy\").\n\nHowever, there is a divergence in clinical practice regarding the preferred reliever for individuals with mild asthma. While some guidelines advocate for a combination of inhaled corticosteroid and formoterol as the preferred option, others still consider short-acting bronchodilators acceptable in mild cases (source: \"Asthma: confirming the diagnosis and the stepwise approach to controller therapy\"). \n\nIn summary, while a consensus exists against using short-acting relievers alone in asthma treatment, specific recommendations may differ regarding the introduction of these medications in conjunction with other treatments for mild asthma.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
